### What is Epsilon GC?

Epsilon implements the full GC interface the JVM expects, but its collection logic is empty. It allocates objects by bumping a pointer through the heap and tracks nothing for reclamation. There are no GC threads, no barriers, no pauses.

```bash
# Enable Epsilon GC (experimental, requires unlock)
java -XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions \
     -XX:+UseEpsilonGC \
     -Xmx256m \
     -jar interest-calc.jar
```

```text
Epsilon behavior:
  Allocation: bump pointer (fastest possible)
  Collection: none
  Pauses: zero
  When heap is full: OutOfMemoryError → JVM stops
```

### How do you use Epsilon for benchmarking?

Epsilon removes GC from the picture entirely, so microbenchmark numbers reflect only allocation and your code logic.

```java
package com.bank.benchmark;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.math.MathContext;

public class InterestBenchmark {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        BigDecimal total = BigDecimal.ZERO;

        // Calculate compound interest for 1 million accounts
        for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
            BigDecimal principal = BigDecimal.valueOf(100_000 + i);
            BigDecimal rate = BigDecimal.valueOf(0.065);
            BigDecimal interest = principal.multiply(rate,
                MathContext.DECIMAL64);
            total = total.add(interest);
        }

        long elapsed = System.nanoTime() - start;
        System.out.printf("Total interest: %s%n", total);
        System.out.printf("Time: %d ms%n", elapsed / 1_000_000);
    }
}
```

```bash
# Benchmark with Epsilon (no GC noise)
java -XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions \
     -XX:+UseEpsilonGC \
     -Xmx512m \
     -cp . com.bank.benchmark.InterestBenchmark

# Compare with G1 (includes GC noise)
java -XX:+UseG1GC \
     -Xmx512m \
     -cp . com.bank.benchmark.InterestBenchmark
```

```text
Epsilon: Time: 234 ms (pure computation + allocation)
G1:      Time: 267 ms (includes 33 ms of GC pauses)
```

### How do you measure allocation rate with Epsilon?

Run a known workload with a fixed heap and see how fast it fills. This gives you a precise bytes-per-operation figure.

```bash
# Measure allocation rate
java -XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions \
     -XX:+UseEpsilonGC \
     -Xmx1g \
     -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError \
     -XX:HeapDumpPath=/tmp/epsilon-heap.hprof \
     -Xlog:gc*:file=epsilon.log \
     -jar interest-calc.jar
```

```text
epsilon.log:
  [0.001s] Heap: 1024M reserved, 0M committed
  [0.500s] Heap: 1024M reserved, 512M committed  (filling...)
  [0.987s] Heap: 1024M reserved, 1024M committed
  [0.988s] OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space

Calculation:
  1 GB filled in 0.987 seconds
  Allocation rate: ~1.01 GB/sec
  If workload processed 500,000 accounts:
    Allocation per account: 1 GB / 500,000 = ~2 KB/account
```

### When is Epsilon useful for short-lived programs?

A small tool, a one-shot batch job, or a serverless function that finishes before the heap fills gets maximum throughput with zero pauses.

```java
package com.bank.tool;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.Files;
import java.nio.file.Path;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class AccountValidator {

    // Short-lived tool: validate account file and exit
    public static void main(String[] args) throws IOException {
        Path file = Path.of(args[0]);
        long invalidCount;
        try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(file)) {
            invalidCount = lines
                .filter(line -> !isValidAccount(line))
                .count();
        }
        System.out.println("Invalid accounts: " + invalidCount);
        // Program exits -- heap is reclaimed by OS
    }

    private static boolean isValidAccount(String line) {
        return line != null && line.length() >= 10;
    }
}
```

```bash
# Short-lived tool with Epsilon: fastest possible, zero GC overhead
java -XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions \
     -XX:+UseEpsilonGC \
     -Xmx128m \
     -cp . com.bank.tool.AccountValidator accounts.csv
```

### How does Epsilon expose memory leaks?

Running under Epsilon makes leaks obvious: anything that a real collector would normally reclaim now accumulates until the JVM dies, so a leak shows up immediately.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class LeakyCache {

    // Under Epsilon, this fills the heap fast because nothing is freed
    private static final Map<String, byte[]> cache = new HashMap<>();

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int i = 0;
        while (true) {
            cache.put("key-" + i, new byte[10_000]);
            i++;
        }
        // Under G1: runs forever (GC reclaims... oh wait, it can't
        //   because the map holds everything -- still a leak, but
        //   slower to notice)
        // Under Epsilon: OutOfMemoryError in seconds -- leak obvious!
    }
}
```

```bash
# Epsilon makes the leak fail fast
java -XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions \
     -XX:+UseEpsilonGC \
     -Xmx256m \
     -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError \
     -cp . com.bank.service.LeakyCache
```

```text
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.OutOfMemoryError: Java heap space
Dumping heap to /tmp/java_pid12345.hprof
# Heap dump reveals the HashMap holding everything
```

### How is Epsilon different from other collectors?

| Aspect | Epsilon | Serial | G1 | ZGC |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Collects garbage? | No | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Pauses | Zero | Full STW | Bounded STW | Sub-ms |
| Allocation speed | Fastest (bump pointer) | Fast | Fast | Fast + barrier |
| Long-running? | No (heap fills) | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Use case | Benchmark, analysis | Tiny heaps | General | Low-latency |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is accidentally leaving Epsilon enabled in a production service that runs indefinitely.

```bash
# WRONG: Epsilon in production -- service crashes when heap fills
java -XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions \
     -XX:+UseEpsilonGC \
     -jar transfer-service.jar
# CRASH: OutOfMemoryError after minutes/hours

# CORRECT: Epsilon only for measurement, G1/ZGC for production
java -XX:+UseG1GC -jar transfer-service.jar
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer treats Epsilon as a precise laboratory instrument. At National Bank of India, Epsilon was used to measure the interest-calculation routine: running it under a fixed heap showed exactly how many bytes the routine allocated per account, revealing far more intermediate objects than necessary. The team optimized the routine and then ran production under G1.

```bash
# Workflow: Epsilon for measurement, real collector for production
# 1. Measure allocation rate with Epsilon
java -XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions -XX:+UseEpsilonGC \
     -Xmx1g -Xlog:gc* -jar interest-calc.jar

# 2. Optimize based on measurement
# 3. Run production with appropriate collector
java -XX:+UseG1GC -XX:MaxGCPauseMillis=100 \
     -Xmx4g -jar interest-calc.jar
```
